  // ── PRIORITY RETENTION CONCEPTS (PSM I) ──────────────────────────────
  retentionGapConcepts: [
    {"label": "Developers Own the Sprint Backlog", "parentTopic": "Developers Accountabilities", objective: "2.1", "keyword": "The Sprint Backlog belongs to the DEVELOPERS: they create it during Sprint Planning (the Sprint Goal, the selected Product Backlog items and the plan for delivering them) and keep it updated through the Sprint as they learn more. The Product Owner proposes how the product could increase its value and helps clarify items but does not assign tasks, and the Scrum Master does not write or manage the plan. The Developers also adapt their plan each day toward the Sprint Goal. Test: if a question asks who decides how the work gets done, the answer is the Developers."},
    {"label": "Only the Product Owner Can Cancel a Sprint", "parentTopic": "Sprint Timeboxes and Cancellation", objective: "1.13", "keyword": "A Sprint may be cancelled only if the Sprint Goal becomes OBSOLETE, and ONLY THE PRODUCT OWNER has the authority to cancel it. The decision is the Product Owner's alone. The Sprint Goal is the test: obsolete goal, possible cancellation; goal still valid, Sprint continues."},
    {"label": "The Three Commitments and What Each Belongs To", "parentTopic": "Commitments: Product Goal, Sprint Goal and Definition of Done", objective: "1.12", "keyword": "Each artifact has ONE commitment that gives it focus: the PRODUCT GOAL belongs to the Product Backlog, the SPRINT GOAL belongs to the Sprint Backlog, and the DEFINITION OF DONE belongs to the Increment. The Product Goal is the long-term objective and there is one at a time; the Sprint Goal is the single objective for the Sprint, set in Sprint Planning; the Definition of Done is the formal description of the Increment's quality. Mixing them up is a classic trap: the Sprint Goal is not the commitment of the Increment."},
    {"label": "Definition of Done Applies to Every Increment", "parentTopic": "Increment and Definition of Done", objective: "1.11", "keyword": "Work is part of an Increment only when it meets the DEFINITION OF DONE; an item that does not meet it cannot be released or even presented at the Sprint Review and returns to the Product Backlog. The Definition of Done is set by the organization if a standard exists (all Scrum Teams follow it at minimum), otherwise by the Scrum Team. Several Scrum Teams on one product must define and comply with the SAME Definition of Done. Multiple Increments may be created within a Sprint."},
    {"label": "Timebox Maxima for a One-Month Sprint", "parentTopic": "Sprint Timeboxes and Cancellation", objective: "1.13", "keyword": "For a ONE-MONTH Sprint the maximums are: Sprint Planning 8 hours, Sprint Review 4 hours, Sprint Retrospective 3 hours, Daily Scrum 15 minutes (always 15, whatever the Sprint length). The Sprint itself is one month or less, and a new Sprint starts immediately after the previous one ends. For shorter Sprints, the events are usually shorter. These are maximums, not targets: an event may finish sooner once its purpose is met."},
    {"label": "Scrum Master: Accountable for Effectiveness, Serves Rather Than Manages", "parentTopic": "Scrum Master Accountabilities", objective: "2.2", "keyword": "The Scrum Master is accountable for establishing Scrum as defined in the Scrum Guide AND for the Scrum Team's effectiveness. They are a true leader who SERVES the Scrum Team and the wider organization: coaching the team in self-management and cross-functionality, removing impediments (or causing them to be removed), and ensuring events happen and stay within the timebox. They do not assign work, run the Daily Scrum on the team's behalf or act as a project manager. The Daily Scrum belongs to the Developers."},
    {"label": "Product Owner: One Person, Accountable for Value and the Backlog", "parentTopic": "Product Owner Accountabilities", objective: "3.1", "keyword": "The Product Owner is ONE PERSON, not a committee, accountable for maximizing the value of the product. They are accountable for effective Product Backlog management: developing and communicating the Product Goal, creating and clearly communicating items, ordering them, and ensuring the backlog is transparent. They may delegate the work to others but remain accountable. Those wanting to change the Product Backlog can do so by trying to convince the Product Owner, and the organization must respect the Product Owner's decisions."},
    {"label": "Empiricism: Transparency, Inspection, Adaptation", "parentTopic": "Scrum Theory: Empiricism and the Three Pillars", objective: "1.1", "keyword": "Scrum rests on EMPIRICISM (knowledge comes from experience and decisions from what is observed) and LEAN thinking. The three pillars are TRANSPARENCY, INSPECTION and ADAPTATION, and they depend on each other: inspection without transparency is misleading, and inspection without adaptation is pointless. Each event is an opportunity to inspect and adapt an artifact. The Scrum Values (commitment, focus, openness, respect, courage) are what make the pillars work for a team."}
  ],

